    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, I can provide more details on that.\n\n1. **Pseudoscientific Correlation**: In the 1950s, policymakers and segregationists frequently cited IQ tests as evidence of inherent racial differences in intelligence. Universities like Stanford and the University of Southern California produced research purported to show systematic racial differences in these tests, often arguing that these differences justified separate facilities. Figures like Madison Grant and Harry Laughlin authored handbooks and studies that reinforced these inequalities by stressing biological determinism. They frequently referenced Mendelian genetics to claim certain \"racial qualities,\" such as intelligence or physical strength, were inherited and therefore distinct. Policymakers tried to present these theories as scientific results from supposedly credible institutions and experts, thereby lending them an air of legitimacy. The Social Darwinism ideology, which extolled the \"survival of the fittest,\" also played into their narratives, portraying segregation as a \"natural\" means to ensure that races would thrive in their own 'proper' environments.",
